  "reasoning": "The user wants a villa. I have a stair calculation already done for rise=3.6, which implies a floor-to-floor height of 3.6m between two levels. Let me design a two-story villa.\n\nSite and volumes:\n- Let me place the site at west=0, east=14, south=0, north=12 (meters).\n- Volumes: main house volume covering west=2 to east=12, south=2 to north=10 (width=10m, depth=8m). Two floors.\n\nLevels:\n- ground = 0.0 (floor top at 0)\n- upper = 3.6 (floor top at 3.6)\n- roof level maybe = 7.2 (top of upper volume)\n\nWait, levels is floor-top world elevation. Let me set:\n- level_g = 0.0 (ground floor top)\n- level_u = 3.6 (upper floor top)\n\nFor volume bottom/top: bottom is the floor elevation of that storey's slab top (the floor you stand on), top is the next level's elevation. So lower volume bottom=level_g=0, top=level_u=3.6. Upper volume bottom=level_u, top=level_u+3.6=7.2? But levels must be referenced by name. Let me define a third level \"roof_deck\" = 7.2.\n\nStair: origin is SW corner of stair footprint. Footprint width=2.2 (across), depth=3.9 (along +z direction). Direction \"+z\" means flight runs along +z. I'll place stair inside volume near north side. Let me set origin=[5.5, 6.0]. Then along=3.9 in z goes from 6.0 to 9.9, north of volume=10, fits. Across=2.2 in x goes from 5.5 to 7.7, within volume east=12.\n\nArrival rect relative to origin: arrival_rect = [1.05, 2.25, -1.2, 0.0] meaning in (across, along) coordinates: across from 1.05 to 2.25, along from -1.2 to 0.0. Hmm along = -1.2 to 0 means exit is at z = origin_z + (-1.2 to 0) = 6.0-1.2=4.8 to 6.0. That's the upper exit.\n\nWait let me re-read. relative exit. Let me think: direction +z, the landing rect is at far +z end. arrival_rect z from -1.2 to 0.0 relative to origin. That's odd, exit landing on upper floor is at z=origin? Let me interpret as: arrival_rect is the area where you exit onto the new floor; x [1.05,2.25], z [-1.2,0]. So exit is at origin corner z=0 (relative), meaning absolute z=6.0, x=origin_x+1.05=6.55 to 7.75. Hmm but x=7.75 must be within volume east=12 yes.\n\nBut actually the stair rises to level_u=3.6, exit at upper floor slab top 3.6, and arrival area z ~ 4.8 to 6.0.\n\nLet me set origin_x=5.5 so arrival x in [6.55, 7.75], origin_z=6.0 so arrival z in [4.8, 6.0]. Fine.\n\nBut wait the stair footprint itself: void_rect covers z [0,3.9] = absolute 6.0 to 9.9. That must be open void in upper floor slab so person can rise. The arrival is outside that void (z<6). Good.\n\nNow room zones:\nGround floor volume = lower:\n- living room zone: west=2, east=7, south=2, north=6\n- dining/kitchen zone: west=7, east=12, south=2, north=6\n- entry hall: west=2, east=7, south=6, north=10? but stair occupies x 5.5-7.7, z 6-9.9. So hall west=2,east=5.5 south=6 north=10. And stair room east.\n- bathroom/storage: west=7.7,east=12, south=6,north=10.\n\nUpper floor volume same rect, top=roof_deck.\n- bedrooms: two or three.\n\nWalls: partition walls on ground and upper.\n\nOpenings: exterior windows on faces, entry door on front face (-Z south face? face front = -Z side). Volume faces: front is -Z (south), back is +Z (north), left is -X (west), right is +X (east).\n\nEntry door on front (south) face at x around 3.5, width 1.2, height 2.4.\n\nRoutes: ground route points from door to living and to stair origin area.\n\nRoofs: lower volume roof? Actually two-storey building typically has one roof over upper volume. The lower volume top is level_u (the floor of upper). Does lower volume need roof entry? \"每个体量恰有一个 roofs 条目\". Hmm. If I have two volumes stacked, the lower one's top is upper's bottom (floor). The rule says each volume must have exactly one roofs entry. So lower volume type \"none\"? Actually we can do a flat roof walkable? It's covered by upper. Let me make lower roof type flat with material but it's interior. Or use a single volume for both floors and room_zones per level. That's simpler!\n\nLet me use a single volume covering both floors: bottom=level_g=0, top=roof_deck=7.2. Then one roof (gable or flat). room_zones assigned to level lower/upper.\n\nBut slab thickness handles floor between. Levels: lower=0, upper=3.6, roof_deck=7.2. Volume bottom=lower, top=roof_deck. Wall top auto = next level's underside? The rule says wall top reduces by slab. For interior this is fine.\n\nOpenings host=volume with face. Good.\n\nRoof: gable over whole roof_deck, or flat walkable terrace. For villa, gable looks nice. ridge_axis x.\n\nStair volume references this volume id.\n\nI'll proceed.",
